videos.script:
An app for both phones can be built four ways. {{four}} You can write it twice, once in Kotlin and once in Swift. You can write it once in React Native or Flutter. You can share only the logic, with Kotlin Multiplatform. Or you can put a website inside an app.

The names hide one question. Who draws the screen? {{draws}} The platform's own buttons, an engine the app carries with it, or a web page. And when the app needs the camera, how does the shared code reach it? {{reach}}

This lesson answers both for each approach. Then it shows one small screen of the planner in Kotlin, Swift and React Native, side by side. {{three}} Last, you run lesson 1's planner again, unchanged, and watch a screen that needs a browser fail on your computer. {{fail}} Logic travels between platforms. Screens do not. That difference is what the next lesson's choice is made of.
